{"type":"route","meta":{"status":200,"headers":{"cache-control":"public, max-age=3600","content-type":"text/plain; charset=utf-8","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/llms-full.txt/layout,_N_T_/llms-full.txt/route,_N_T_/llms-full.txt"}},"body":"# Amantrika — full text\n\n> Amantrika turns an Indian wedding invitation into a single shareable link: a themed invitation website with RSVPs, directions, a countdown and a photo gallery.\n\nGenerated from the site source. Index: http://localhost:3000/llms.txt\n\n---\n\n# About Amantrika\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/about · Updated 8 August 2026\n\nWe create wedding invitations in the form of a website that will make a long-lasting impression on your guests without burning your pockets.\n\n## A box under the bed\n\nMy mother keeps a box under her bed. In it are wedding cards — her own, her sister's, cards from weddings in the family going back more than thirty years. Thick paper, gold foil, red envelopes, some of them with the rice still caught in the fold.\n\nShe has never thrown one away. Nobody in our family has.\n\nThat is the thing about an Indian wedding invitation. It is not a notification. It is the object that says a family thought of you, sat down, wrote your name, and came to your house to hand it over. The information on it — the date, the hall, the muhurat — stops being useful the day after the wedding. The card stays for decades.\n\n## What the card cannot do\n\nI started thinking about this properly while helping with a cousin's wedding.\n\nThe venue changed eleven days before the ceremony. Four hundred cards had already gone out. What followed was a fortnight of phone calls and a WhatsApp message that some people saw and some did not — and on the day, a handful of guests still arrived at the original hall.\n\nMeanwhile my aunt was counting RSVPs on the back of an envelope, relatives abroad were being sent photographs of the card, and my grandmother — who reads Hindi far more comfortably than English — was handed a card printed entirely in English.\n\nNone of that is a failure of the card. It is a failure of asking a printed object to do things a printed object cannot do: change, count, travel, and speak more than one language.\n\nTo give every family an invitation that carries the warmth of the card they would have printed — and does everything the card never could.\n\n## Digital elegance and a modern invitation experience\n\nSix things we set out to get right, and hold ourselves to.\n\n### A unique experience\nBreak away from the monotony of traditional physical and video invitations. Opt for something modern, and make an impression by crafting moments that reflect your own style.\n\n### Multilingual invitations\nCraft your invitation in the language that resonates with you and your guests — so the grandmother who reads Hindi, Tamil or Gujarati more comfortably than English is not handed something she has to squint at.\n\n### Smart and tech-savvy\nVenue details, directions and transport options integrated into the invitation itself, so planning is effortless for your guests rather than a series of phone calls to you.\n\n### High-quality design\nEvery detail thoughtfully crafted, down to the border rules and the typeface each script is set in. The invitation should look like it was made for you, because it was.\n\n### An eco-friendly approach\nChoosing digital is a conscientious step away from the paper and waste of a full card run — a celebration aligned with a greener tomorrow.\n\n### Genuinely affordable\nSay goodbye to the hassle and expense of traditional cards. A pocket-friendly alternative that does not ask you to compromise on style or quality.\n\n## Beyond the basics\n\nWe create invitations as special as your day. We are your dedicated partner in crafting unforgettable moments, with a focus on simplicity, elegance and timeless aesthetics.\n\n### Quick and effortless\nCreate your invitation in minutes. Enter your details and it is done — no back-and-forth with a designer, no waiting on a proof.\n\n### Support and service\nFrom creating the invitation to sending it, there is a person at the other end of the email — not a ticket queue.\n\n### User-friendly by design\nThe platform is easy to use. Anyone can create an invitation without any technical knowledge, on a phone, at the kitchen table.\n\n### Professional quality\nHigh-quality designs that make your invitation stand out — and reach relatives on the other side of the world the same evening.\n\n## What we built\n\nAmantrika is the other half of the card. The invitation as something that lives at a link: it updates when the venue moves, it collects the RSVPs into a list instead of a scroll, it opens in the language the person reading it is most comfortable in, it gives directions, and afterwards it holds the photographs.\n\nIt is not a replacement for the box under the bed. Most families we talk to print cards for the people they visit in person, and send a link to everyone else. That seems right to us. **The card is the gesture; the link is the logistics.**\n\n## What we will not do\n\nSome of these are unusual to state publicly. We would rather be held to them.\n\n### Your invitation is yours\nWe never publish anyone's invitation, photographs or guest details in a public gallery without being asked first — and asking means a checkbox you tick, not a line buried in terms nobody reads.\n\n### Guests are not tracked for our benefit\nHosts can see how many people opened the invitation, because that is genuinely useful when you have sent it to four hundred people. Nobody outside your dashboard sees anything, and no advertising script ever loads on a guest's page.\n\n### No invented social proof\nIf you do not see testimonials on this site yet, it is because we would rather show none than write fake ones.\n\n### The link never changes\nWhatever you shared keeps working — through edits, through payment, through the venue moving the week before.\n\n### No dark patterns at checkout\nNo pre-ticked boxes, no countdown timers invented to rush you, no price that changes when you hesitate.\n\n## Four steps, start to share\n\nCreate your own invitation with us and share it with just four easy steps.\n\n### Choose a template\nPick the theme that fits your tradition and the feeling of your wedding.\n\n### Fill in the details\nNames, ceremonies, venue, photographs. It saves as you type.\n\n### Preview your invitation\nSee exactly what your guests will see, on a phone, before anything is final.\n\n### Share a link\nOne link, sent on WhatsApp. It updates whenever you edit it.\n\n## Early, and building in the open\n\n### The beginning: A cousin's wedding, and a venue that changed\nFour hundred printed cards, eleven days' notice, and no way to tell everyone. That is the problem Amantrika was started to solve.\n\n### First build: Amantrika goes live\nA small set of themes, a builder, and a link you could send. Enough to prove the idea to the families who used it.\n\n### Now: Rebuilt from the ground up\nOne codebase, real accounts, real RSVPs, and a design system of eight themes built for Indian traditions rather than adapted from western templates.\n\n### Next: Twelve languages, properly done\nNot a translate button — a first-class part of the invitation, with names transliterated rather than translated and the right typeface for every script.\n\nWe are building for Hindu-tradition Indian weddings first, because that is the tradition we know best and the one where getting the details wrong is most obvious. Engagements, receptions, baby showers, birthdays, anniversaries and griha pravesh sit on the same foundation and follow.\n\n## Start your invitation\n\nPick a theme, add your details, and see your own names in it. It is free to build — you only pay when you publish.\n\n---\n\n# Changelog\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/changelog · Updated 8 August 2026\n\nEverything here has shipped and is live. Anything still to come is on the\n[roadmap](/roadmap) — the two are kept apart on purpose, so a plan is never\nmistaken for a promise.\n\n## August 2026\n\n### 8 August: Showcase gallery\n\nA public [gallery](/showcase) of real invitations, shared only with their hosts'\nexplicit permission.\n\nConsent is a single unticked checkbox in the builder, and ticking it only makes\nan invitation *eligible* — we still review before anything appears. What gets\npublished is never your live invitation: it is a separate copy with the venue\naddress reduced to a city, and phone numbers, gift and payment details, and your\nentire guest list removed. First-names-only is on by default. Withdraw at any\ntime and the copy is deleted immediately, not queued for later.\n\n### 8 August: Partner programme\n\nPlanners, printers and studios can now [apply](/signup?as=agent) to build\ninvitations for their clients.\n\nEach partner gets a referral code, a dashboard covering every invitation they\nmanage, and a commission ledger that updates the moment a client pays.\nApplications are reviewed by hand — a partner cannot touch a client's\ninvitation until they are approved.\n\n### 8 August: Invitations for every occasion\n\nAmantrika is no longer wedding-only. Engagements, receptions, anniversaries,\nbirthdays, baby showers, naming ceremonies, housewarmings, graduations and\ncorporate events each get their own ceremony presets and vocabulary.\n\nThe builder reshapes itself around what you are celebrating — a birthday asks\nabout one celebrant, not two partners.\n\n### 7 August: Your own photographs\n\nUpload your own photographs instead of placeholders. They are stored securely,\ndelivered from a global CDN, and framed automatically in your theme's arch,\nscallop or polaroid style.\n\nJPG, PNG, WebP, AVIF and GIF up to 25 MB each. Uploads go straight from your\nbrowser to storage, and only you — and your partner agent, if you have one — can\nadd or remove them.\n\n### 7 August: Accounts, RSVPs and a real dashboard\n\nInvitations now live in a proper account rather than in one browser.\n\nSign in to see total views, unique visitors and a fourteen-day trend for each\ninvitation, alongside RSVPs, headcounts and meal preferences as they arrive.\nImport your whole guest list at once and copy a personalised link per guest.\n\nBlessings can be moderated before they appear, and the RSVP form and blessing\nwall can each be switched off entirely.\n\n## A note on privacy\n\nTwo decisions from this month are worth stating plainly, because they are the\nkind that are easy to get wrong quietly.\n\n### Your guests are not our data\n\nGuest names, phone numbers, RSVP messages and blessings stay in your\ninvitation's own records. They are never sent to any analytics service.\n\nWe count how many people opened an invitation using a salted hash of the\nvisitor's device that is regenerated daily — enough to tell you fifty people\nlooked rather than five, and not enough to follow anyone from one day to the\nnext.\n\n### The showcase never links to your invitation\n\nA featured invitation is always a sanitised copy at its own address. Nothing in\nthe gallery links back to a family's real invitation, and the copy carries no\nRSVP form or blessing wall — a sample must not collect anybody's details.\n\n---\n\n# Contact us\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/contact · Updated 8 August 2026\n\n## Email us\n\n**hello@amantrika.com**\n\nThat address reaches a person, not a queue. We answer within a working day, usually sooner.\n\nUseful things to include, if they apply:\n\n- The link to your invitation, or the slug you chose\n- The phone or browser you were using when something went wrong\n- A screenshot, if it is a display problem\n\n## If your wedding is this week\n\nSay so in the subject line. Anything with a date attached goes to the front.\n\n## Wedding planners, printers and photographers\n\nIf you build invitations for your own clients, there is a partner arrangement — wholesale pricing, multiple invitations under one account, and the ability to hand an invitation over to the couple so they manage their own RSVPs. Email us with a line about your business and the city you work in.\n\n## Press and partnerships\n\nSame address. Please put \"Press\" or \"Partnership\" in the subject.\n\n### On the contact form\nThere is no form on this page yet. We would rather send you to an address that definitely reaches us than to a form that might not — the previous version of this site had a contact form that rendered perfectly and submitted nowhere, and we are not repeating that.\n\n---\n\n# How Amantrika works\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/how-it-works · Updated 8 August 2026\n\n## Step one: Build it\n\nYou start with a theme and your details: names, the ceremonies, the venue, a photograph or two. The form asks for things in the order a guest reads them, and it saves as you type — you can stop halfway through and come back.\n\nEverything is optional except the parts a guest actually needs. An invitation with names, a date, a venue and one event is a complete invitation. The story, the gallery, the travel notes and the FAQ are there if you want them and absent if you do not.\n\n## Step two: Change the theme whenever you like\n\nThe content of your invitation and the way it looks are stored separately. That means switching themes does not touch a word of what you have written — you can build the whole invitation, then look at it in four different themes and pick.\n\nThis is deliberate. Most people only know which theme is right once they see their own names in it.\n\n## Step three: Preview it before you pay\n\nBefore publishing, your invitation is fully viewable — the real thing, with a watermark across it. You can send that preview to your family and argue about the wording with everyone looking at the same page.\n\nWhen you publish, the watermark comes off. **The link does not change.** Anything you shared as a preview keeps working.\n\n## Step four: Share one link\n\nYou get a single URL for the invitation. Send it on WhatsApp, put it in an email, print a QR code on the physical card — it is the same link everywhere.\n\nWhat guests see when they open it:\n\n- The invitation itself, designed for a phone first\n- The full schedule, with each function's time and venue\n- A map and one-tap directions\n- A countdown to the next function\n- Numbers they can tap to call\n- An RSVP form that takes about fifteen seconds\n\nNo app, no login, and no third-party tracking script loading on their phone.\n\n## Step five: Collect RSVPs\n\nResponses arrive as a list rather than as replies scattered across family groups. You can see who has responded, how many are coming to which function, and who has not answered yet — which is the part that lets you send a reminder to the twenty people who need one instead of to all four hundred.\n\nYou can export the whole thing to a spreadsheet, because at some point the caterer will ask for a number and you will want to hand them something.\n\n## Step six: Update it when things change\n\nVenues move. Timings shift. The entrance changes to Gate 3 the night before.\n\nEdit the invitation and every guest who already has the link sees the new version. Nothing needs re-sending, and nobody ends up at the old address with a printed card in their hand.\n\n## Afterwards: Afterwards\n\nThe invitation does not have to disappear the day after the wedding. It stays up for a while by default, and can be kept permanently as the place the photographs live — the link your guests already have, still working years later.\n\nThat is the part of the box under the bed we are actually trying to build.\n\n---\n\n# Roadmap\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/roadmap · Updated 8 August 2026\n\nThis is what we intend to build next, roughly in order. There are no dates: a\ndate given to a customer is a promise, and we would rather keep the promises we\nmake on the [changelog](/changelog), where everything listed already works.\n\n### Nothing on this page works yet\n\nIf something here is important to your wedding, plan as though it will not\nexist. Everything that does exist is on the [changelog](/changelog).\n\n## Next\n\nThe things we are actively working on.\n\n### In progress: Real payments\n\nCard, UPI and netbanking through a payment provider, replacing the demo checkout.\nInvoices, refunds, and partner commissions paid out on a schedule rather than\ntracked on a ledger.\n\n### In progress: Faster invitations\n\nPages should open instantly on a mid-range phone over patchy mobile data,\nbecause that is what most of your guests are using. Caching what rarely changes,\nloading photographs more intelligently, and reducing what has to be fetched\nbefore the first paint.\n\n### Next: Guest messaging\n\nSend the invitation from Amantrika itself instead of copying links by hand.\nPersonalised links per guest, delivery and open tracking, and reminders to the\npeople who have not yet replied — without turning into something that spams your\nrelatives.\n\n## Soon after\n\n### Planned: Your invitation in your language\n\nHindi, Gujarati, Marathi, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Bengali, Punjabi, Malayalam,\nOdia and Urdu — with a machine-drafted first pass you review and correct, proper\ntransliteration for names, and script-aware fonts.\n\nAn elder reading the invitation in the language they think in is worth more than\nalmost any feature on this list.\n\n### Planned: Your own domain\n\nPoint a domain you own at your invitation, so the link you share on WhatsApp is\nentirely yours.\n\n### Planned: After the wedding\n\nAn invitation should not stop being useful once the day is over. A private album\nyour guests can add their own photographs to, and an archive page the invitation\ngracefully becomes rather than simply expiring.\n\n### Planned: More occasions, more traditions\n\nMore themes, and more traditions represented properly rather than recoloured —\neach with its own motifs, vocabulary and ceremonies.\n\n## Under consideration\n\nIdeas we think are good but have not committed to. Tell us if one of these is\nthe thing standing between you and a finished invitation.\n\n  <IconCard icon=\"mandap\" title=\"Seating and tables\">Arrange tables, assign guests, and let each guest find their own seat from their personalised link.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"AcrossOceans\" title=\"Livestream\">A built-in place for relatives who cannot travel to watch the ceremony.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"mithai\" title=\"Gift registry\">A registry, and a cleaner way to receive blessings as gifts than posting a UPI ID.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"invitation-scroll\" title=\"Printed cards\">Order matching printed cards from the invitation you have already designed, for the people you visit in person.</IconCard>\n\n## What we will not do\n\nWorth saying plainly, because the absence of these is a decision rather than an\noversight.\n\n  <IconCard icon=\"KeptPrivate\" title=\"Sell or share your guest list\">Your guests' names and numbers are yours. They are never sold, shared, or used to advertise anything.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"CardToLink\" title=\"Require an app\">Guests open a link in the browser they already have. No download, no login, no OTP — ever.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"nazar\" title=\"Put ads on your invitation\">Your invitation will never carry advertising for us or anybody else.</IconCard>\n  <IconCard icon=\"GuidingLamp\" title=\"Take your invitation down without warning\">If anything ever has to change about how long invitations stay up, you will hear it from us directly and well in advance.</IconCard>\n\n---\n\nMissing something? [Tell us](/contact) — what couples and planners actually run\ninto shapes this list far more than our own guesses do.\n\n---\n\n# Sharing your wedding invitation on WhatsApp without it getting lost\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/sharing-your-wedding-invitation-on-whatsapp · The Amantrika Team · 2 August 2026 · Guides\n\n> What to write around the link, when to send it, and why a bare forwarded URL gets ignored by exactly the guests you most want to reach.\n\nThe invitation is finished, the link works, and you send it. Then half the family says they never got it.\n\nThey did. It arrived in a group at 11pm between a forwarded video and someone's good-morning image, and it scrolled away. This is a delivery problem, not an invitation problem, and it is mostly fixable with how you send.\n\n## Never send a bare link\n\nA URL on its own reads as a forward. Forwards get ignored, particularly by the older relatives whose attendance you care most about.\n\nTwo or three lines is enough. Name yourselves, say what it is, say what you want:\n\n> Namaste Uncle, Aunty —\n> Ananya and Rohan are getting married on 14 February, and we would be so happy to have you with us.\n> Here are all the details, including the schedule and directions:\n> [link]\n> Do let us know how many of you will be able to come.\n\nThat takes twenty seconds longer to send and roughly triples the chance of a response.\n\n### Send it from the right person\nAn invitation from the bride's mother to her own siblings lands very differently from the same link forwarded by a cousin. For the guests who matter most, it is worth the sender being the person they expect to hear from.\n\n## Order of sending\n\n### Close family, individually, first\nBefore anything goes into a group. Nobody in the immediate family should learn the date from a group forward.\n### Family groups next\nThis is your reach. Post it with a message, not alone, and post it at a time people are awake — mid-morning or early evening, not midnight.\n### Friends and colleagues after that\nIndividually or in small groups. Colleagues in particular respond better to a direct message than to being added to something.\n### A second wave about a month before\nSame link, short message: \"Just a reminder — the wedding is in four weeks and here are the details again.\" People forget. This is normal and this message is not an imposition.\n\n## Check the preview before you send it\n\nWhen you paste the link, WhatsApp fetches a preview card — a photo, the couple's names, a short description. Send it to yourself first and look at what appears.\n\nIf the preview shows a plain grey box or the wrong photograph, fix that before the link goes anywhere. The preview is what most people register in a busy group; a link with a good card gets opened far more than one without.\n\n### Previews cache\nWhatsApp remembers the preview it fetched the first time a link was shared. If you change the photo afterwards, older chats may keep showing the old card for a while. Get the preview right before the first send rather than after.\n\n## Groups: a few practical notes\n\n- **Do not create a new group for the wedding** unless you intend to run it. An unmanaged wedding group becomes a support queue.\n- **Pin the message** in family groups you control, so the link stays reachable when the conversation moves on.\n- **Expect the link to be forwarded onward.** It will reach people you did not send it to. If that is a problem, use personalised links per family rather than one shared URL.\n\n## What to send on the day itself\n\nOne short message on the morning of each function, to the people attending it, with the timing and the venue link. Not the full invitation again — just the thing they need in the next two hours.\n\n> Today's sangeet starts at 7:30pm at Hotel Suryagarh. Directions: [link]. See you there!\n\nThis is the message guests are most grateful for, and it is the one almost nobody sends.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Should I send the invitation to a group or individually?**\n\nBoth, in that order. Family groups first for reach, then individual messages to elders and to anyone whose attendance matters enough that you would notice their absence.\n**How many times is it acceptable to send the invitation?**\n\nTwice to everyone — on announcement and about a month before — plus a targeted third message only to people who have not responded. More than that reads as pressure.\n\n---\n\n# Choosing a wedding invitation theme that still looks right in six months\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/choosing-a-wedding-invitation-theme · Prachi Jain · 28 July 2026 · Inspiration\n\n> How to pick a visual style for your invitation: what to match it to, what to ignore, and the tests worth running before you commit.\n\nMost theme advice is about taste, which is not much help when two families have four opinions. What follows is about the decisions that turn out to matter afterwards.\n\n## Match the tradition before the aesthetic\n\nThe first filter is not \"what do I like\". It is \"what does this wedding actually look like\". A Nikah invitation and a Punjabi wedding invitation are not interchangeable with a colour swap — the motifs, the scripts and the greeting lines are different, and guests notice immediately when they are wrong.\n\nStart by narrowing to the themes built for your tradition, then choose within that set. It cuts a paralysing decision down to three or four options, and it removes the risk of the mistake that actually embarrasses people.\n\n## Match the formality of the function\n\nThe other useful filter is how formal the invitation should feel — and this varies by function, not by wedding.\n\n- **The wedding itself** is the most formal thing you will send. This is where ornate, traditional and restrained all work.\n- **Sangeet and mehndi** invitations can be much lighter. A playful theme that would be wrong for the wedding is right here.\n- **The reception** sits somewhere between, and often goes to a wider list including colleagues, which pushes it back towards formal.\n\nIf you are sending one invitation covering everything, it should sit at the formality of the most formal function on it.\n\n## Four tests worth running before you commit\n\n### Look at it on a phone, at arm's length\nNot on your laptop. Almost every guest will open it on a phone, many outdoors, several with the brightness down. If the names are not immediately readable at arm's length, the theme is too delicate.\n### Screenshot it and send it to yourself\nGuests screenshot invitations constantly to forward them. Some themes survive that and some turn into a grey smear. Test it.\n### Put the longest name on it\nCompound surnames, both sets of parents, a long venue name. Ornate frames that look beautiful with \"Ananya & Rohan\" often break down with the full formal names.\n### Show it to someone from the older generation\nNot for approval — for legibility. If your grandmother has to hold the phone at three distances to read the date, the type is too small or the contrast too low, and she is exactly the guest you cannot afford to lose.\n\n### Try more than one\nBecause content and theme are stored separately, switching themes costs nothing and loses nothing. Build the invitation once, then look at it in three or four themes before deciding. Most people change their mind after seeing their own names in it, not before.\n\n## What matters less than people think\n\n**Matching the decor.** Guests receive the invitation months before they see the mandap, and almost never compare them. Spend the energy on legibility instead.\n\n**The trend.** Invitations are looked at for a few weeks and then, occasionally, for decades. A theme chosen because it is current tends to date exactly as fast as the trend that produced it.\n\n**Animation.** A nice opening is a nice opening. It is not what makes an invitation good, and on a poor connection it is the first thing to become an obstacle. If a theme's animation delays the names appearing, that is a defect, not a feature.\n\n## What matters more than people think\n\n**Contrast.** The single most common problem: gold text on cream looks exquisite on a designer's monitor and disappears on a phone in daylight.\n\n**How the schedule reads.** Most guests come to the invitation for one piece of information — when and where. A theme that makes the schedule beautiful but hard to scan is working against the thing the invitation is for.\n\n**The script your names are set in.** If any part of the invitation is in Hindi, Tamil, Gujarati or Urdu, the theme needs a typeface that renders that script properly. A theme that falls back to a system font for the Devanagari line will look visibly unfinished next to its English.\n\n## A reasonable way to decide\n\nNarrow by tradition. Narrow again by formality. Put your real names and your real venue into the two or three that remain. Look at all of them on a phone, one after another, and pick the one where you can read everything without trying.\n\nThat is usually the right answer, and it is rarely the one that looked best in the theme gallery.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Can I change the theme after I've built the invitation?**\n\nIn Amantrika, yes — the content and the visual theme are stored separately, so switching one does not touch the other. Nothing you have written is lost when you change how it looks.\n**Should the invitation match the wedding decor?**\n\nLoosely. Guests see the invitation months before the decor and rarely hold the two side by side. Matching the tradition and the formality matters far more than matching a colour.\n**What colours should I avoid on a wedding invitation?**\n\nAnything that fails to hold up in a screenshot on a dim phone screen — very dark palettes and low-contrast pastels both suffer. Regional associations matter too: white alone reads as mourning in several Indian traditions.\n\n---\n\n# How to collect wedding RSVPs without three hundred WhatsApp replies\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/collect-wedding-rsvps-without-whatsapp-chaos · The Amantrika Team · 4 July 2026 · Planning\n\n> Why Indian weddings are hard to count, what to ask for instead of a yes or no, and how to get a headcount the caterer can actually work from.\n\nCounting guests at an Indian wedding is harder than at most weddings, for a reason that has nothing to do with technology: invitations are extended to families, attendance is decided by families, and the number changes until the week of the event.\n\nNo RSVP system fixes that. A good one just stops you from doing the arithmetic by hand.\n\n## Why the WhatsApp method breaks down\n\nIt works fine up to about forty guests. Past that it fails in specific, predictable ways.\n\n- **Replies arrive across a dozen chats and three family groups.** There is no list; there is a scroll.\n- **\"We'll definitely come!\" is not a number.** How many is \"we\"? Are the children coming? For which functions?\n- **Responses to the wrong person.** The bride's mother gets half of them, the groom's cousin the other half, and neither has the whole picture.\n- **Late changes overwrite nothing.** A \"make that four instead of six\" sent three weeks after the original message is simply gone.\n- **You cannot tell who has not replied**, because absence of a reply looks identical to a reply you scrolled past.\n\n### The cost of getting this wrong\nCatering is usually priced per plate with a guaranteed minimum. Over-count and you pay for empty seats; under-count and there is a real, visible shortage at the reception. This is the most expensive spreadsheet at the wedding.\n\n## Ask for the right things\n\nThe single biggest improvement is not the tool. It is what you ask. A yes/no question produces answers you cannot cater from.\n\n| Ask this | Instead of | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| How many adults and how many children? | How many guests? | Children's plates are often priced differently, and \"guests\" gets interpreted inconsistently |\n| Which functions will you attend? | Are you coming? | Different functions, different catering, different headcounts |\n| Name of the person responding | *(nothing)* | Otherwise you get a count with no idea whose family it is |\n| Phone number | *(nothing)* | You will need to reach someone about arrival timings |\n| Arrival date, for out-of-town guests | *(nothing)* | Drives hotel blocks and airport pickups |\n| Meal preference, if you're catering separately | *(nothing)* | Only ask if it changes what the caterer does; otherwise it's noise |\n\nKeep it to what you will act on. Every extra field costs you completed responses, and a field you never look at costs you responses for nothing.\n\n## Make responding take fifteen seconds\n\nGuests do not abandon RSVP forms because they are undecided. They abandon them because the form asked for eight things while they were standing in a queue.\n\n### Put the form in the invitation itself\nNot a link to a separate site, not an email address. The guest is already looking at the invitation; the form should be the next thing they scroll to.\n### Pre-fill everything you already know\nIf you sent a personalised link, their name and phone number should already be filled in. What remains is a headcount and a function or two — genuinely a few seconds.\n### Don't require an account\nNo login, no OTP, no app. Every step between the guest and the submit button costs you responses, and the elderly relatives you most need a count for are the first to drop off.\n### Confirm on screen, immediately\n\"Thank you, we have you down for four\" prevents the double-submission that inflates your count.\n\n## Chase the non-responders, not everyone\n\nThe reminder that works is targeted. A message to the whole list irritates the two hundred people who already replied and teaches them to ignore the next one.\n\nA workable schedule:\n\n- **On sending:** expect a burst of responses in the first 48 hours. This is most of your eventual total.\n- **Three to four weeks before:** a reminder to people who have not responded. This is the one that recovers the most.\n- **Ten days before:** a final, short message to the remaining non-responders. Give a deadline and mean it.\n- **After the deadline:** stop asking and estimate. Some families will simply arrive.\n\n### The estimate that saves you\nTrack how many invited guests have not responded and assume a share of them attend anyway. Your caterer has seen this a hundred times and will have a number for your region — ask them rather than guessing.\n\n## What to do with the responses\n\nA list of names is not a plan. Before the week of the wedding, turn the responses into four things:\n\n1. **A headcount per function**, which is what the caterer is waiting for.\n2. **An out-of-town list with arrival dates**, which drives hotel rooms and pickups.\n3. **A contact list for the day itself** — the people someone will need to call when a family cannot find the venue.\n4. **A seating or table plan**, if you are doing one, built from confirmed numbers rather than the invitation list.\n\nIf your RSVP responses cannot be exported to a spreadsheet, you will end up retyping them at the worst possible moment. Check that before you start collecting.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**How far in advance should the RSVP deadline be?**\n\nTwo to three weeks before the wedding for most caterers, though ask yours — many need final numbers earlier than couples expect, and some accept adjustments up to 72 hours before.\n**What do I do about guests who never respond?**\n\nAssume a portion never will and plan for it. A targeted reminder to only the non-responders, about ten days before, recovers most of them. Chasing the whole list annoys the people who already replied.\n**Should I ask for a headcount per function?**\n\nYes, if different functions have different catering. A guest attending the sangeet and the reception but not the mehndi is common, and a single yes/no cannot express that.\n\n---\n\n# The ceremonies in a Hindu wedding, and which ones your invitation should name\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/hindu-wedding-ceremonies-explained · The Amantrika Team · 11 June 2026 · Traditions\n\n> From roka to vidaai: what each ceremony is, roughly when it happens, and how to put the schedule on an invitation so guests know where to be.\n\nGuests do not need a theological account of your wedding. They need to know which functions they are invited to, when to arrive, and what to wear. But knowing what each ceremony actually is makes it much easier to write those lines well.\n\nWhat follows is broadly the north Indian sequence. Your family's may differ, and where it does, your family is right and this article is not.\n\n## Before the wedding days\n\nThese usually happen weeks or months earlier, and often carry their own separate invitations.\n\n**Roka.** The first formal agreement between the families. Small, usually only close family, and increasingly the point at which the couple's names are announced publicly.\n\n**Sagai or engagement.** The exchange of rings, and often the first function with a real guest list. Many families now send their first proper invitation for this.\n\n**Tilak.** The bride's family visits the groom's home to formally accept him. Traditionally men only, though this has relaxed considerably.\n\n### Why these get their own invitation\nRoka and engagement guest lists are usually a fraction of the wedding list, and often a different set of people entirely. Sending one invitation covering everything means most recipients reading about functions they are not invited to.\n\n## The wedding days\n\n### Ganesh puja and mandap muhurat\n\nThe first ritual of the wedding proper: a prayer to Ganesha for an obstacle-free ceremony. Family only, and usually not something guests are invited to.\n\n### Haldi\n\nTurmeric paste applied to the bride and groom by family members, at their respective homes, usually on the morning of the day before the wedding.\n\n*What guests need to know:* wear something you do not mind staining permanently. Say this on the invitation. People will not believe you, and it will still help.\n\n### Mehndi\n\nHenna applied to the bride's hands and feet, and to the other women in the family. Typically a long, unhurried daytime function with music.\n\n*What guests need to know:* start time and how long it runs, because guests drift in and out over several hours rather than arriving at once.\n\n### Sangeet\n\nMusic, dancing, and family performances that were rehearsed with more seriousness than anyone admits. The loudest and least formal function.\n\n*What guests need to know:* the real start time, and whether dinner is served.\n\n### Baraat\n\nThe groom's procession to the venue, with a band and, in some families, a horse. The single most timing-sensitive item on the entire invitation.\n\n*What guests need to know:* where it assembles and when it leaves, because guests joining the baraat need to be at the assembly point, not the venue.\n\n### Varmala or jaimala\n\nThe exchange of garlands when the couple meets at the venue. Short, photographed heavily, and the moment most guests want to be present for.\n\n### Kanyadaan and pheras\n\nThe core rituals: the bride's parents give her hand, and the couple circles the sacred fire — usually seven times, sometimes four, depending on tradition. This is the marriage itself.\n\n*What guests need to know:* the muhurat time, honestly stated. If the pheras begin at 1:20 am, put that on the invitation. Guests who expected an evening's end at eleven will not thank you for the surprise.\n\n### Vidaai\n\nThe bride's departure from her family home. Emotionally the heaviest moment of the wedding, and one many families choose not to list as a public function at all.\n\n## Putting the schedule on an invitation\n\nThe mistake is listing every ceremony to everyone. A guest invited to the reception alone, reading a six-function schedule, has to work out which line applies to them — and some will simply guess wrong.\n\n### List only what this guest is invited to\nIf your list splits into groups — full-wedding guests, wedding-day-only guests, reception-only guests — the invitation should reflect that split, not paper over it.\n### Give every function its own venue line\nFunctions at home and functions at the hall are routinely confused. Repeat the venue for each one even when it feels redundant.\n### State the time you mean\nNot the auspicious time, not the aspirational time. The time a guest should walk in.\n### Add the dress note where it matters\nHaldi and mehndi especially. One line each is enough.\n\n### The practical benefit of a digital invitation here\nFunction timings are the thing most likely to change, and they change late. A schedule on a link can absorb that. A schedule on four hundred printed cards cannot.\n\n## Regional variation is the norm, not the exception\n\nThe sequence above will look wrong to a good number of families, and correctly so. A Tamil wedding centres on the muhurtham and the thaali; a Bengali wedding on the gaye holud and the subho drishti; a Gujarati wedding on the mandvo and the pithi; a Punjabi wedding adds the chooda and the kalire.\n\nIf you are writing an invitation for a tradition you did not grow up in, ask the family rather than a template. The names of the ceremonies are the part guests read most closely, and getting one wrong is noticed immediately.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**How many days does a Hindu wedding last?**\n\nThe main functions usually run across two to four days. Rituals before that — roka, engagement, tilak — are often weeks or months earlier and get their own invitations.\n**Which ceremonies should be listed on the invitation?**\n\nEvery function the guest receiving it is invited to, with its own date, time and venue. A guest invited only to the reception should not have to work out which of six listed events applies to them.\n**Do all Hindu weddings follow the same sequence?**\n\nNo. The sequence below is broadly north Indian. South Indian, Bengali, Gujarati, Maharashtrian and Punjabi weddings differ in the rituals, their names and their order — sometimes substantially.\n\n---\n\n# Digital or printed wedding invitations: an honest comparison\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/digital-vs-printed-wedding-invitations · The Amantrika Team · 9 May 2026 · Planning\n\n> What each format actually does well, what it costs, and why most Indian families end up using both — with a rule for deciding who gets which.\n\nThe choice is usually framed as tradition against convenience. That framing is not very useful, because the two formats are good at genuinely different things and most families need both.\n\n## What each format is actually good at\n\nRead that table honestly and the answer is not \"digital wins\". It is that the printed card is an object and the digital invitation is a service. They are not competing at the same thing.\n\n## The printed card is a gesture, not a delivery mechanism\n\nThe reason a printed card still matters in an Indian wedding is that giving one is an act. You visit the house, you hand it over, you are given tea, you sit for twenty minutes. The card is the reason for the visit, not the content of it.\n\nNobody who receives a card that way is checking it for the venue's Maps pin. They already know where the hall is, or they will ask.\n\n### Where the printed card genuinely fails\nIt fails at everything that happens after it is delivered. Venue changed? Reprint. Timings shifted? Reprint. Want to know how many are coming? Start calling. Cousin in Toronto? Courier, and hope.\n\n## The digital invitation is the logistics layer\n\nEverything the card cannot do, a link can, because a link points at something that is allowed to change.\n\n- **It updates.** The entrance moves to Gate 3 the night before; you edit it and every guest sees the new one.\n- **It counts.** RSVPs arrive as a list rather than as ninety WhatsApp replies you have to tally by hand.\n- **It navigates.** One tap opens Maps. This matters more than anything else on the day itself.\n- **It travels.** Relatives abroad get it the same evening, in the same form as everyone else.\n- **It survives.** After the wedding it becomes where the photographs live.\n\n## How most families actually split it\n\nThe rule that works, and the one nearly every family arrives at independently:\n\n### Print for the people you will visit\nElders, close family, the families you have a relationship with that involves showing up at their door. Usually a much smaller number than the full guest list — often fifty to a hundred cards rather than four hundred.\n### Send digitally to everyone else\nColleagues, college friends, extended family, everyone abroad. This is the majority of the list and the group for whom a card is a courier problem rather than a gesture.\n### Put the link on the printed card too\nA QR code or a short URL on the card, so even the people who got the physical card have the schedule, the map and the RSVP form in their pocket. This is the step that makes both formats work together instead of competing.\n\nThat third step is the one families skip and later wish they hadn't. The card in the drawer cannot tell anyone that the sangeet moved to 8 pm. The link on it can.\n\n## On budget\n\nThe honest position: a digital invitation does not save money if you were going to print anyway, and most families are. What it changes is the *shape* of the cost. Print cost scales with your guest list and with every change; a digital invitation does not care whether you send it to eighty people or eight hundred, and it does not charge you for changing your mind.\n\nWhere families do save is in printing fewer cards, not none — going from four hundred to a hundred, and sending the rest as a link.\n\n## The one thing that decides it\n\nIf the invitation needs to *do* something — collect responses, give directions, absorb a change, reach someone overseas — it needs to be digital, regardless of what else you print.\n\nIf it needs to *mean* something to someone who will keep it in a box, print it and take it to their house yourself.\n\nMost weddings need both, which is why the argument about which is better has never quite resolved.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Is a digital wedding invitation acceptable for elders?**\n\nWidely, yes — provided it arrives properly. A link forwarded through a family group with no message reads as careless. The same link sent personally, with two lines of context, does not. The format is rarely the objection; the delivery is.\n**How much does a printed Indian wedding card cost?**\n\nIt varies enormously by city, paper and box. What is predictable is the shape of the cost: it scales with every additional guest, and reprinting after a change means starting over.\n**Can I use both a printed card and a digital invitation?**\n\nThat is what most families do. Print for the elders and the families you visit in person, digital for everyone else and for all the logistics.\n\n---\n\n# How to word an Indian wedding invitation, in English and Hindi\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/how-to-word-a-wedding-invitation · Prachi Jain · 2 April 2026 · Guides\n\n> Who invites whom, where the parents' names go, and ready-to-use wording for the wedding, sangeet, mehndi and reception in both languages.\n\nInvitation wording is where families get stuck, because it is not really a writing problem. It is a question of who is inviting whom, and that is a family decision with the words attached.\n\n## Decide who is inviting first\n\nEverything else follows from this. There are three common arrangements.\n\n**The bride's family invites.** The oldest convention, still the most common. The parents' names lead, the daughter is named as their daughter, and the groom is named with his parents.\n\n> Mr. and Mrs. Rakesh Sharma\n> request the pleasure of your company\n> at the wedding of their daughter\n> **Ananya**\n> with\n> **Rohan**\n> son of Mr. and Mrs. Vijay Mehta\n\n**Both families invite together.** Increasingly the default, and the least fraught. Both sets of parents appear, and the couple's names sit together.\n\n> Mr. and Mrs. Rakesh Sharma\n> and Mr. and Mrs. Vijay Mehta\n> invite you to celebrate the marriage of\n> **Ananya and Rohan**\n\n**The couple invites.** Correct, modern, and increasingly common when the couple is paying. The parents are usually still named, out of respect rather than obligation.\n\n> Together with our families,\n> **Ananya and Rohan**\n> invite you to share in the joy of their wedding\n\n### A note on late parents\nWhere a parent has passed away, the usual form is \"daughter of the late Shri Rakesh Sharma and Smt. Sunita Sharma\". Some families prefer to place a small \"in loving memory\" line at the foot of the invitation instead. Both are correct; the family decides.\n\n## The parts of the invitation, in order\n\nAn invitation answers five questions, in this order, and the reason the order matters is that guests stop reading once they have what they need.\n\n| Part | What it says | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| The invitation line | Who is inviting | *Mr. and Mrs. Rakesh Sharma request the pleasure…* |\n| The names | Who is getting married | *Ananya with Rohan* |\n| The date and time | When | *Sunday, 14 February 2027, from 7:00 pm* |\n| The venue | Where | *The Leela Palace, Diplomatic Enclave, New Delhi* |\n| The request | What to do next | *Kindly confirm your presence by 1 February* |\n\nAnything beyond these five — the dress code, the hashtag, the parking note — belongs further down, not woven into the invitation itself.\n\n## Hindi wording you can use directly\n\nIf your guests read Hindi more comfortably than English, the Hindi should be the invitation, not a translation printed underneath it as an afterthought.\n\n**The invitation line:**\n\n> सादर निमंत्रण\n> श्री एवं श्रीमती राकेश शर्मा\n> अपनी सुपुत्री **अनन्या** के शुभ विवाह के अवसर पर\n> आपको सपरिवार आमंत्रित करते हैं\n\n**A commonly used blessing line:**\n\n> शुभ विवाह\n> ॐ गणेशाय नमः\n\n**The request to attend:**\n\n> आपकी उपस्थिति ही हमारा आशीर्वाद है।\n> *Your presence is our blessing.*\n\n### Names are transliterated, not translated\n\"Ananya Sharma\" becomes \"अनन्या शर्मा\" — the same name in a different script. It never becomes a Hindi word that means something similar. This sounds obvious and is the single most common mistake in machine-translated invitations.\n\n## Wording for the other functions\n\nEach function has its own tone, and the invitation reads better when the wording matches it rather than repeating the wedding's formality five times.\n\n### Mehndi\n\nInformal, usually daytime, usually the bride's side.\n\n> Bring your hands and your appetite —\n> **Ananya's mehndi**\n> Friday, 12 February, from 11 am\n> at the Sharma residence\n\n### Sangeet\n\nThe loudest function gets the least formal invitation.\n\n> An evening of songs, dancing and questionable choreography.\n> **Sangeet · Saturday 13 February, 7 pm**\n\n### Haldi\n\n> Wear something you don't mind ruining.\n> **Haldi · Saturday 13 February, 10 am**\n\n### Reception\n\nBack to formal, and often a wider guest list than the wedding itself.\n\n> Mr. and Mrs. Vijay Mehta\n> request the pleasure of your company\n> at a reception in honour of\n> **Ananya and Rohan**\n\n## Small things that are worth getting right\n\n- **Spell out the date.** \"Sunday, 14 February 2027\" is unambiguous in a way that \"14/02/27\" is not, particularly for guests abroad.\n- **Give a start time you mean.** If the muhurat is at 8:40 pm, say so. Guests who arrive at a stated 7 pm and wait two hours remember it.\n- **Say what you want back.** \"Kindly confirm by 1 February\" gets responses. \"RSVP\" alone does not.\n- **Name a person to contact,** not just a number. People are far more likely to call \"Kunal (brother of the groom)\" than an unattributed mobile number.\n- **Check every name with the family it belongs to.** Spelling, initials, honorifics. This is the one error nobody forgets.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**Should the invitation come from the couple or from the parents?**\n\nTraditionally from the parents of the bride, and increasingly from both sets of parents together. If the couple is hosting themselves, inviting in their own name is entirely correct — the phrasing simply changes from 'request the pleasure' to 'together with our families, we invite you'.\n**Whose name goes first on an Indian wedding invitation?**\n\nOn an invitation sent by the bride's family, the bride's name goes first; on one sent by the groom's family, the groom's. When both families invite jointly, alphabetical order or the order the families agree on is fine — there is no rule worth arguing over.\n**Do I need Hindi on the invitation at all?**\n\nOnly if some of your guests read it more comfortably than English. For many families that is exactly the case for the grandparents' generation, which is reason enough.\n\n---\n\n# How to create a wedding invitation website in about fifteen minutes\n\nSource: http://localhost:3000/blog/step-by-step-guide-to-create-wedding-website · Prachi Jain · 14 March 2026 · Guides\n\n> A practical walkthrough for building a digital wedding invitation, from gathering details to sharing the link with three hundred relatives.\n\nMost of the work in a digital invitation is not the building. It is deciding what goes on it. This guide covers both, in the order that wastes the least time.\n\n## Gather five things before you start\n\nYou can build an invitation without these and fill them in later, but having them open in another tab turns an evening into a coffee break.\n\n1. **Full names, spelled the way you want them printed.** Including how each family's surname should appear.\n2. **The event schedule.** Every function, with its date, start time and venue. Mehndi, haldi, sangeet, the wedding itself, the reception — whichever apply.\n3. **The venue address, as it appears on Google Maps.** Not the address as your family says it. The one Maps recognises, so the directions link actually works.\n4. **One photograph you both like.** Landscape if you have it. A phone photo in decent light beats a badly-lit professional one.\n5. **Two phone numbers** for guests who need to ask something — usually a sibling or a cousin, rarely the couple.\n\n### On photographs\nIf your pre-wedding shoot has not happened yet, do not wait for it. Publish with a good photo now and swap it in later. The link stays the same, so nothing you have already sent breaks.\n\n## Write the invitation before you pick a theme\n\nThis is the step people skip, and it is the one that determines whether the invitation reads well. Themes are quick to try on. Words are not.\n\nStart with the line that names who is inviting. In most Indian families, the invitation comes from the parents, not the couple, and the wording carries that:\n\n> Mr. and Mrs. Rakesh Sharma request the pleasure of your company at the wedding of their daughter Ananya to Rohan, son of Mr. and Mrs. Vijay Mehta.\n\nThen the ceremony details, then the venue, then the RSVP request. If you want a more detailed treatment of phrasing — including the Hindi equivalents and what changes when the couple invites in their own name — read the [wording guide](/blog/how-to-word-a-wedding-invitation).\n\n## Choose a theme that matches the function, not your mood\n\nEvery theme in Amantrika reads the same content, so switching one for another loses nothing. That means you can afford to try several. What actually helps is narrowing by tradition and by the feeling of the wedding itself.\n\nA few honest observations from watching people choose:\n\n- **Dark themes photograph badly in screenshots.** Guests screenshot invitations constantly to send onward. A lighter theme survives that better.\n- **A theme with a lot of ornament needs short names.** Long compound names crowd an ornate frame.\n- **Pick for the phone.** Almost nobody will open your invitation on a laptop. Look at it at phone width before deciding.\n\n## Fill in the details, then check them on a phone\n\nEnter the names, the events, the venue and the contacts. The builder saves as you type, so there is nothing to lose by stopping halfway.\n\n### Names and date\nThe couple's names and the main wedding date. This is what the invitation is built around, and it's what appears in the WhatsApp preview when someone shares the link.\n### Events\nAdd each function with its own time and venue. Guests attending only the reception should be able to see exactly when to arrive without doing arithmetic.\n### Venue and directions\nPaste the Maps location. The invitation renders a static map and a directions link — one tap and the relative in the auto-rickshaw is on their way.\n### Photos and story\nOptional, and genuinely optional: the invitation looks finished without them. Add them if you have them.\n### RSVP\nDecide whether you want a headcount per function or just one total. Per-function is more useful and takes guests ten seconds longer.\n\nThen open it on your own phone before anyone else sees it. Read it as a guest would: can they tell what day, what time, where, and what to do next? If any of those takes more than a glance, fix that before sharing.\n\n## Share it once, in the right place\n\nSend the link on WhatsApp, because that is where your guests are. A few things worth knowing:\n\n- **Send it to family groups, not one by one.** The link is the same for everyone unless you are using personalised links for RSVP tracking.\n- **Say something in the message.** A bare link gets ignored; two lines of context gets opened.\n- **Expect a second wave.** People forget. Sending the same link again a month before the wedding is normal and effective.\n\n### What happens after the wedding\nThe invitation does not need to disappear. It becomes the place the photographs live, and the thing your guests still have the link to. Whether you keep it that way is your decision, but the option is worth knowing about while you are still deciding what to build.\n\n## What usually goes wrong\n\nThree failures account for most of the problems we see:\n\n- **The venue address is not the Maps address.** Guests end up at a similarly named hall on the other side of the city. Test the directions link yourself.\n- **The timings are aspirational.** If the baraat realistically leaves at nine, do not print seven. Guests plan around what you write.\n- **No contact number.** Something always needs asking. Give people a person to ask.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**How long does it take to create a wedding invitation website?**\n\nAbout fifteen minutes if your names, dates, venue address and one good photo are ready. Gathering those details is what actually takes time — the building is fast.\n**Can I change the venue after sending the invitation?**\n\nYes. The invitation lives at one link, so an edit updates what every guest sees. Nobody needs to be re-sent anything, which is the main thing a printed card cannot do.\n**Do guests need to install an app?**\n\nNo. The invitation opens in whatever browser is already on their phone. There is no login and no download.\n\n---\n"}